WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.550 --> 00:00:10.720
Yes, yes.

00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:19.150
And not only in Russia, but of course in post-Soviet states, yes... 

00:00:20.190 --> 00:00:26.000
many people speak Russian; not just in Ukraine, but also in Kazakhstan...

00:00:26.010 --> 00:00:31.110
and in many other countries Russian is spoken.

00:00:31.420 --> 00:00:41.450
And it is one of the major World Languages, alongside Spanish, Arabic etc.

00:00:42.370 --> 00:00:49.480
I will tell you, it was about the time...

00:00:50.090 --> 00:00:56.000
Yes – it was ten years ago, when I really began focusing on studying Russian.

00:00:56.260 --> 00:01:02.700
Unfortunately, I haven’t practiced my Russian in a long time.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:10.090
When I visited Ukraine twice I was able to hear a little more Russian.

00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:12.610
And so, unfortunately,

00:01:12.940 --> 00:01:21.000
speaking accurately in Russian requires effort.

00:01:21.010 --> 00:01:26.000
And when I don’t study Russian for a prolonged period of time,

00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:34.360
of course I’ll forget words and make many mistakes.

00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:36.050
But for me that’s unimportant. 

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:42.000
I will show people that right now I’m speaking with difficulty,

00:01:42.470 --> 00:01:50.390
but it’s still a huge pleasure for me whenever I get the chance to speak Russian.

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:55.000
And it doesn’t hinder our communication at all, I understand you perfectly...

00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:58.000
...and think there is no need to be afraid of making mistakes.
(Yes)

00:01:58.310 --> 00:02:03.150
They are inevitable, but shouldn’t hinder communication.

00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:08.910
Yes, and even if I focus on speaking Russian,

00:02:09.330 --> 00:02:12.000
I am afraid – no, I am not afraid,

00:02:12.260 --> 00:02:18.520
however I know that I will never speak with zero mistakes, ever.

00:02:19.010 --> 00:02:20.470
Never.

00:02:20.750 --> 00:02:27.760
When it comes to English... I was in international business for many years,

00:02:28.010 --> 00:02:32.800
with people from Sweden, Germany...

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:35.100
And not to mention from Japan.

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:44.370
Well, the Japanese aren’t the best with English,

00:02:44.620 --> 00:02:49.030
but Swedes and Germans are great with English, 

00:02:49.050 --> 00:02:53.280
But nonetheless there will always be some mistakes. 

00:02:53.750 --> 00:03:10.900
These mistakes will, how do you say... “reflect”... reflect the habits of their native languages.

00:03:11.090 --> 00:03:16.050
Of course: here in Canada we have a co-worker from Russia,

00:03:16.050 --> 00:03:21.000
no, not Russian, he’s a Russian-speaking Moldovan,

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:23.000
so post-Soviet country.

00:03:23.130 --> 00:03:26.530
And his English is great, simply great,

00:03:26.580 --> 00:03:29.000
he can express himself perfectly.

00:03:29.130 --> 00:03:35.040
He understands everything, and speaks like any regular Canadian,

00:03:35.470 --> 00:03:38.670
but articles don’t exist for him.

00:03:39.350 --> 00:03:43.900
No, no, there are simply no articles.

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:53.200
And he speaks freely with no articles, and everyone understands him.

00:03:53.200 --> 00:04:00.400
...So if I were to focus on Russian right now and study hard,

00:04:01.100 --> 00:04:11.000
I would like to learn to speak more accurately,

00:04:11.560 --> 00:04:14.790
but even so I know I will never speak with zero mistakes.

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:23.800
And this doesn’t matter, it doesn’t take away the pleasure I find in studying Russian.

00:04:23.820 --> 00:04:25.500
Absolutely. It all depends on your goal.

00:04:25.640 --> 00:04:30.210
After all, you’re not aiming to work as an interpreter at the United Nations

00:04:30.210 --> 00:04:32.000
or become the President’s interpreter.
(Yes)

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:37.000
If that were the case, investing extra effort in speaking flawlessly would be worth it. 

00:04:37.220 --> 00:04:40.450
I’ve been living in the US for 10 years and have been studying English since age 5,

00:04:40.460 --> 00:04:47.260
and I’m sure that I still make many mistakes but people are well-mannered and don’t point them out.

00:04:47.650 --> 00:04:53.030
Russians also make many mistakes in their native language.

00:04:53.030 --> 00:04:56.850
They wouldn’t confuse a word’s case as a foreigner might,

00:04:57.900 --> 00:05:00.050
but word stress, word definitions and so on.

00:05:00.430 --> 00:05:08.260
Language is profound and boundless, like, I don't know... an abyss; so there’s no trouble in making mistakes.

00:05:08.850 --> 00:05:15.100
Question: If I want to improve my speaking...

00:05:15.100 --> 00:05:18.020
...and make less mistakes,

00:05:18.950 --> 00:05:29.000
what would you suggest, how could you help me make less mistakes?

00:05:30.130 --> 00:05:37.070
My suggestion is this: hire a teacher – someone who can explain your mistakes to you,

00:05:37.070 --> 00:05:43.860
or at least, a native speaker who isn’t your spouse or close friend,

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:45.900
because that can cause conflicts,

00:05:45.920 --> 00:05:51.800
and ask them to communicate with you regularly, write your mistakes down and then tell you about them.

00:05:51.950 --> 00:05:58.480
Then, I think, you will need to direct your attention to these mistakes.

00:05:58.780 --> 00:06:02.000
So let’s say you constantly make mistakes with word cases,

00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:07.190
since we tend to make the same old mistakes over and over.

00:06:07.550 --> 00:06:12.950
You will need to stop and say to yourself: hold it right there, here is where I need to change something.

00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:16.130
So, we need awareness and to consciously use our cognitive skills

00:06:16.350 --> 00:06:21.770
to slowly train ourselves to stop making a particular mistake.

00:06:22.430 --> 00:06:26.000
This is the recipe I’ve come up with. What do you think of it?

00:06:26.330 --> 00:06:33.000
Yes... Well, I understand Russian grammar,

00:06:33.010 --> 00:06:37.250
I do, I understand everything, have done a lot of reading.

00:06:37.300 --> 00:06:54.750
But I cannot speak and simultaneously think consciously about the rules of speech,

00:06:55.080 --> 00:07:05.730
so how can I develop speaking reflexively/intuitively?

00:07:06.260 --> 00:07:16.370
And what you said is correct, when I’m studying, I don’t know, Arabic or any other language, 

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:22.140
I will be speaking to a tutor via the Internet.

00:07:22.480 --> 00:07:31.140
And every time the tutor will make a list of my mistakes. 

00:07:31.960 --> 00:07:42.310
And I will be able to review these conversations... no, just the lists...

00:07:44.690 --> 00:07:50.120
What I mean to say is I have a recording of the conversation.

00:07:50.250 --> 00:07:57.330
And I see that every week I make the same mistakes.

00:07:57.340 --> 00:08:02.840
And every time the tutor will correct me,

00:08:02.950 --> 00:08:09.800
but the next lesson I’ll make that same mistake again.

00:08:10.120 --> 00:08:18.200
Even though I know the mistake, and I’ve been corrected.

00:08:18.500 --> 00:08:24.500
And how do you, how should I say, how do you fix that?

00:08:24.500 --> 00:08:26.610
How do you correct that? 

00:08:27.050 --> 00:08:28.000
Correct, yes.

00:08:28.120 --> 00:08:33.000
Well, Steve, I think we shouldn’t forget about awareness and repetition.

00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:36.380
Right, you may make the same mistake twice, thrice, a fifth time,

00:08:36.380 --> 00:08:40.080
but on the seventh you will stop and say to yourself: “No, something’s wrong”. 

00:08:40.350 --> 00:08:45.450
So there still needs to be some form of control over what you’re saying.

00:08:45.450 --> 00:08:51.000
Actually, I agree with your method too. You say we need to get used to a language,

00:08:51.900 --> 00:08:53.190
You say we need to get used to a language, do a lot of listening and reading,
(yes)

00:08:53.190 --> 00:09:01.200
so the language begins to feel native and your ear becomes trained to intuitively catch the mistakes you make.

00:09:01.200 --> 00:09:09.670
So, there needs to be total combination of a huge amount of listening, reading and constant mindfulness.

00:09:09.670 --> 00:09:15.000
As a result we get something that’s natural and effortless for you.

00:09:16.000 --> 00:09:19.100
I also think you shouldn’t be afraid,

00:09:19.100 --> 00:09:26.480
and to admit ... I admit that I won’t be able to speak without mistakes.

00:09:27.100 --> 00:09:38.480
But I also know, like you said, little by little I will gradually develop new habits.

00:09:38.820 --> 00:09:49.750
For example, take cases: the instrumental case is the most simple (“s nami”, “s vami”),

00:09:49.750 --> 00:09:55.210
..because it's slightly different:

00:09:55.710 --> 00:09:58.000
"-i", "-e", "-a" (the endings of the other cases) are complicated,

00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:01.100
but “s vami”, “s nami” – those are easy to remember.

00:10:01.100 --> 00:10:06.550
And my brain will be able to create phrases with these cases that sound natural.

00:10:07.300 --> 00:10:20.020
Over time, the number of these natural-sounding phrases with correct grammatical structure will slowly increase.

00:10:20.320 --> 00:10:32.330
Of course, it’s necessary for somebody to correct you

00:10:32.370 --> 00:10:38.400
or you yourself should mark the difficulties you encounter. 

00:10:38.400 --> 00:10:43.260
But there are difficulties still. So don’t...

00:10:45.000 --> 00:10:47.300
...don’t get to worked up over it, you know?

00:10:47.300 --> 00:10:48.840
Yes, there’s no need to worry too much,

00:10:49.250 --> 00:10:52.200
you must to understand what you need to work on.
(To worry)

00:10:52.200 --> 00:10:54.800
Someone may not even know they’re making a mistake,
(Yes)

00:10:54.800 --> 00:10:57.500
is it “sprosit’ vam” or “sprosit’ vas” (to ask you)?
(Yes)

00:10:57.600 --> 00:10:59.060
The correct way is “sprosit’ vas”, 

00:10:59.060 --> 00:11:02.010
but all my students say “sprosit’ vam”.

00:11:02.010 --> 00:11:05.340
If I don’t tell them that’s wrong, they’ll never find out.

00:11:07.000 --> 00:11:11.300
So the delight of having a teacher lies in their ability to direct you on what you need to work on,

00:11:11.490 --> 00:11:13.220
And that’s about it.
(Yes)

00:11:14.270 --> 00:11:15.170
Yes

00:11:15.570 --> 00:11:27.590
Okay, well, I hope that next time we speak Russian I will be able to improve slightly.

00:11:27.590 --> 00:11:32.500
Your Russian is wonderful, it can only become even better.

00:11:32.940 --> 00:11:45.000
Yes, nevertheless, I think your videos and channel are very encouraging, how do you say that?

00:11:45.070 --> 00:11:47.550
Thank you, We could say: “motiviruyut” (motivating, encouraging).

00:11:47.550 --> 00:11:50.570
Motivating – that’s what's most important. 

00:11:50.570 --> 00:11:51.940
(Thank you very much)
OK, thank you, thank very much, farewell!

00:11:51.940 --> 00:11:53.140
(Thank you, it was a pleasure, farewell!)

00:11:53.140 --> 00:11:54.000
Farewell, Goodbye!
 
It was a pleasure, farewell!

00:11:54.000 --> 00:11:55.000
It was a pleasure

